## 5. Component and Template Library

### 5.1 Library organization

Provide a dedicated library surface accessible from the Builder, with at least:

- Built-in Elements
- Built-in Components
- Built-in Section Templates
- Built-in Header Templates
- Built-in Footer Templates
- Built-in Page Templates
- My Components
- My Section Templates
- My Header Templates
- My Footer Templates
- My Page Templates
- Recently Used and Favorites

The user must be able to search, preview, insert, duplicate, rename, categorize, and delete their own saved items, subject to dependency checks.

### 5.2 Save actions

When one or more nodes are selected on the Canvas, offer context-appropriate actions:

- Save as Component
- Save as Section Template
- Save as Header Template (when the selection is intended as a header)
- Save as Footer Template (when the selection is intended as a footer)
- Save as Page Template (page-level action)
- Duplicate
- Copy / Paste

The save flow should request only essential metadata: name, optional description, and category/tags. The system should infer the template type from the selected save action rather than asking the user to choose it again.

### 5.3 Insert and edit semantics

- Inserting a template creates an editable instance in the target page.
- A normal inserted template is independent from its saved source after insertion unless the user explicitly chooses a linked/synchronized component mode.
- A reusable Component may support linked instances, but the UI must clearly identify linked behavior and the scope of edits.
- Provide an explicit Detach/Convert to Local action for linked instances.
- Do not silently propagate edits from a saved template to existing pages.
- Deleting a saved template must not delete already inserted independent instances.
- Warn before deleting a component definition that is still referenced by linked instances.

### 5.4 Versioning and compatibility

Each saved component/template should include:
- Stable ID
- Type
- Name and description
- Schema version
- Builder engine compatibility/version
- Responsive settings
- Theme-token references and local overrides
- Component tree/document payload
- Created/updated timestamps
- Optional revision history

Versioning requirements:
- Saving changes to a reusable definition creates a new revision or updates it according to the documented revision policy.
- Existing linked instances must have predictable update behavior.
- Provide a review/preview step before applying a newer component revision to existing linked instances.
- Migrations must preserve unknown or unsupported properties where feasible.
- Do not use exported HTML as the editable source of truth; retain the native Builder document/component representation.

