## 6. Global and Local Header/Footer System

### 6.1 Page-level modes

Each page must independently configure Header and Footer modes:

| Mode | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Global | Render the site's selected shared Header/Footer |
| Local | Render a page-specific Header/Footer component |
| None | Render no Header/Footer in that position |

Header and Footer are configured independently. A page may use Global Header with Local Footer, Local Header with None Footer, and so on.

### 6.2 Site-level settings

Add a Site Layout / Global Components area in Site Settings with:
- Global Header definition/selection.
- Global Footer definition/selection.
- Ability to maintain multiple saved Header/Footer designs.
- Explicit selection of which design is currently active as the site's global default.
- Preview at Desktop, Tablet, and Mobile.
- Save and publish status consistent with the existing Save/Publish model.

The system may support multiple named global variants (e.g. Main Header, Minimal Header, Transparent Header), but each page's Global mode must resolve to a deterministic selected variant. Do not infer a variant from page name.

### 6.3 Page Management integration

Add Header and Footer settings to each page's settings surface.

Recommended controls:

- Header: Global / Local / None
- Footer: Global / Local / None
- If Global: show the selected global variant and provide a shortcut to manage it.
- If Local: show the page-specific component and actions to create, edit, replace, detach, or save as a template.
- If None: show a clear empty state and an action to enable one.

Default behavior for newly created pages:
- Inherit the site's configured Global Header and Global Footer by default.
- Allow the user to change either setting independently.
- Page templates may define an initial choice, but applying a page template must show whether it will change the page's Header/Footer modes.

### 6.4 Local Header/Footer creation

A Local Header/Footer may be:
- Created from blank.
- Created from a built-in Header/Footer template.
- Created by copying the current Global Header/Footer.
- Created from one of the user's saved Header/Footer templates.

Copying Global to Local must create an independent local definition. It must not remain linked unless the user explicitly selects a linked mode.

A Local Header/Footer may be saved as a reusable Header/Footer Template at any time.

### 6.5 Editing and propagation rules

| User action | Expected result |
|---|---|
| Edit Global Header | Affects all pages currently configured to use that Global Header after the next successful publish |
| Edit Local Header on Page A | Affects Page A only |
| Change Page A from Global to Local by copying | Creates independent local copy; other pages unchanged |
| Change Page A from Global to None | Header removed from Page A only |
| Replace Page A Local Header with a saved template | Page A uses a new independent instance unless linked behavior is explicitly selected |
| Edit a saved template | Does not silently modify existing independent instances |
| Update a linked component revision | Requires clear preview/confirmation and affects only linked instances selected for update |

Apply the same rules to Footer.

### 6.6 Canvas representation

The editor must visually distinguish:
- Global Header region
- Local Header region
- Page content region
- Local Footer region
- Global Footer region

Provide clear labels or badges in the editor, but do not make the published site show editor-only labels.

When a Global component is selected from a page canvas, make it clear that editing it changes the shared global definition. Offer a shortcut to open the Global Component editor.

### 6.7 Publishing behavior

Publishing is site-wide and atomic, consistent with the existing publishing specification.

- Save persists source documents and component definitions to the user's Google Drive.
- Publish/Update builds all affected routes and shared components from the latest saved source.
- Global Header/Footer changes must be included in every page that references the affected global definition.
- Local Header/Footer changes affect only the owning page.
- Build and validate the complete site output before activation.
- Activate the new immutable build only after all required pages and assets succeed.
- Failed build preserves the previously active site version.
- Rollback restores a prior successful build and its matching shared/local layout output.
- Do not partially activate a site where some pages use new global components and others use old ones.

